From f14b9965bc0c4da0311ead4760bb1d1290d15195 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 01:48:18 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] GlobalStiffnessMatrix: values assembled where hypre computes,
 on the GPU too
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The global matrix of a conforming parallel space whose pattern and exchanges
between the processes are computed once was refused when hypre computes on
a GPU, its values being added on the host: the tangent operator assembled
by kernels was then the product `Pᵀ A P` of hypre at each assembly, which
computes the pattern again each time (about 60 % of the assembly of the
tangent operator of a polycrystal on MI300A).

The setup now gives, for each entry of the global matrix, the list of its
contributions: entries of the local matrix, then received values. A kernel
computes each value as the sum of its contributions in the order of the
list, on the host or on the GPU, where the values of the local matrix are
read and the ones of the global matrix written without copy to the host:
there is one assembly, without concurrent additions. The values are the
ones of the former loop of the host, bit for bit, on the host and on the
GPU. On a GPU, the tangent operator differs from the product of hypre by
round-off errors: the results of the computations on a GPU change
accordingly.

The setup searches the positions of the entries of the local matrix in
the global matrix row by row (the positions of the entries of a row of the
global matrix are kept by column while the entries of the same row of the
local matrix are processed), instead of searching the row of the global
matrix for each entry: on a GPU, one process holds the matrix of millions
of unknowns, and this search took 5.2 s out of the 10 s of the setup of
3.3 million unknowns, 2.0 s now.

The values of the rows owned by other processes are exchanged by non
blocking messages between the processes concerned, given to MPI on the GPU
with GPU-aware MPI. The prolongation and the pattern, read on the host by
the setup, are given back to hypre. The global matrix being the same object
at each assembly, the copies of its values that hypre keeps in it on a GPU
(sorted columns, triangular solves of the Gauss-Seidel relaxations) are
freed by each assembly: hypre would otherwise relax with the values of the
previous assembly.

GlobalStiffnessMatrixTest: no longer refused on a GPU; checksum of the
entries of each global matrix, the same on the host and on the GPU, without
and with GPU-aware MPI (GlobalStiffnessMatrixTest.cmake); Gauss-Seidel
smoother of hypre applied after a second assembly.
